" (https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.783/2013/LectureNotes6.pdf)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.783/2013/LectureNotes6.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1267\nL0@P0: 18.783 Elliptic Curves Spring 2013\nL1@P0: Lecture #6 02/26/2013\nL2@P0: Andrew V. Sutherland\nL3@P0: In this lecture we continue our study of isogenies and introduce the division polynomials.\nL4@P0: Recall that an isogeny is a rational map that is also a group homomorphism. In the last\nL5@P0: lecture we showed that every nonzero isogeny α: E1 → E2 between elliptic curves in short\nL6@P0: Weierstrass form y\nL7@P0: 2 = x3 + Ax + B can be written as\nL8@P0: α(x, y) = \u0012\nL9@P0: u(x)\nL10@P0: v(x)\nL11@P0: ,\nL12@P0: s(x)\nL13@P0: t(x)\nL14@P0: y\nL15@P0: \u0013\nL16@P0: where u ⊥ v and s ⊥ t are pairs of relatively prime polynomials in ¯k[x].1 For any affine\nL17@P0: point (x0, y0) ∈ E1(\nL18@P0: ¯k), we have α(x0, y0) = 0 if and only if x0 is a a root of v(x) (recall that\nL19@P0: v and t always have the same set of roots), and of course α(0) = 0. We defined the degree\nL20@P0: of α to be max{deg u, deg v}, and said that α is separable whenever\nL21@P0: u\nL22@P0: v\nL23@P0: \u00010\nL24@P0: 6= 0.\nL25@P0: 6.1 The kernel of an isogeny\nL26@P0: The polynomial v(x) allows us to determine the points in E(\nL27@P0: ¯k) that lie in the kernel of α.\nL28@P0: Indeed, we have\nL29@P0: ker α = {(x0, y0) ∈ E(\nL30@P0: ¯k) : v(x0) = 0} ∪ {0}.\nL31@P0: If E1 is defined by y\nL32@P0: 2 = f(x) = x3 + Ax + B, then we get one point in ker α for each root\nL33@P0: of v that is also a root of f (these are points (x0, 0) of order 2), two points for every other\nL34@P0: distinct root of v (since α(x0, y0) = 0 implies α(x0, −y0) = −α(x0, y0) = 0), and the point 0\nL35@P0: (the point at infinity). Thus the polynomial v(x) completely determines ker α, and we will\nL36@P0: see later in the course that separable isogenies are effectively determined by their kernel.\nL37@P0: We now wish to show that when α is separable, the number of points in its kernel is\nL38@P0: exactly equal to its degree. We first prove an important intermediate result: every nonzero\nL39@P0: isogeny is surjective.\nL40@P0: Theorem 6.1. Let α: E1 → E2 be a nonzero isogeny. Then α surjects onto E2(\nL41@P0: ¯k).\nL42@P0: Proof. Let α = (u(x)\nL43@P0: v(x)\nL44@P0: ,\nL45@P0: s(x)\nL46@P0: t(x)\nL47@P0: y) be in standard form. Let (a, b) be any nonzero point in E2(\nL48@P0: ¯k),\nL49@P0: and let f be the polynomial u − av. There are two cases:\nL50@P0: Case 1: f has a root x0 ∈ ¯k.\nL51@P0: Pick y0 ∈ ¯k so that (x0, y0) ∈ E1(\nL52@P0: ¯k) (this is possible, since ¯k is algebraically closed). We\nL53@P0: have f(x0) = u(x0) − av(x0) = 0 with v(x0) 6= 0, since u ⊥ v implies f ⊥ v. Thus\nL54@P0: a = u(x0)/v(x0), so α(x0, y0) = (a, b0) ∈ E2(\nL55@P0: ¯k) for some b\nL56@P0: 0 ∈ ¯k. If E2 is defined by the\nL57@P0: equation y\nL58@P0: 2 = x3 + Ax + B, then we have\nL59@P0: b\nL60@P0: 02 = a3 + Aa + B = b2\nL61@P0: .\nL62@P0: Thus b\nL63@P0: 0 = ±b and (a, b) = α(x0, ±y0), so (a, b) lies in the image of α.\nL64@P0: Case 2: f has no roots in ¯k.\nL65@P0: This means that f is constant (since ¯k is algebraically closed). But u and v cannot both\nL66@P0: 1The assumption that E1 and E2 are in short Weierstrass form implies that we are not in characteristic 2\nL67@P0: (and rules out some curves in characteristic 3). Most of the results we will prove can be extended to curves\nL68@P0: in general Weierstrass form and therefore apply to any elliptic curve. Where this is true we will state our\nL69@P0-1: theorems generally, but our proofs will use elliptic curves in short Weierstrass form.be constant, otherwise there would be a point (u/v, y0) ∈ E2(\nL70@P1: ¯k) with infinite pre-image\nL71@P1: in E1(\nL72@P1: ¯k), which is impossible since the kernel of any nonzero isogeny is finite. It follows\nL73@P1: that a is uniquely determined as the ratio of the leading coefficients of u and v.\nL74@P1: Since a is unique, at most two points (a, ±b) do not lie in the image of α (all others fall\nL75@P1: into Case 1). Choose (a\nL76@P1: 0\nL77@P1: , b0) = α(P1) so that (a, b) + (a\nL78@P1: 0\nL79@P1: , b0) 6= (a, ±b); this is possible since\nL80@P1: E1(\nL81@P1: ¯k) and E2(¯k) are infinite. Then (a, b) + (a\nL82@P1: 0\nL83@P1: , b0) lies in the image of α and is equal to\nL84@P1: α(P2) for some P2 ∈ E1(\nL85@P1: ¯k). But then\nL86@P1: α(P1 − P2) = −(a, b) = (a, −b) and α(P2 − P1) = (a, b),\nL87@P1: so (a, b) and (a, −b) are both in the image of α. Therefore α is surjective.\nL88@P1: We now show that the degree of a nonzero separable isogeny is equal to the size of\nL89@P1: its kernel. More generally, we will prove that the separable degree of any nonzero isogeny\nL90@P1: is equal to the size of its kernel. Recall that every inseprable isogeny α can be uniquely\nL91@P1: decomposed as α = αsep ◦ π\nL92@P1: n\nL93@P1: , where αsep is a separable isogeny and π(x, y) = (x\nL94@P1: p\nL95@P1: , yp) is\nL96@P1: the p-power Frobenius map and p is the characteristic of k. The separable degree of α is\nL97@P1: defined to be deg αsep, or simply deg α when α is separable.\nL98@P1: Theorem 6.2. Let α: E1 → E2 be a nonzero isogeny. The cardinality of ker α is equal to\nL99@P1: the separable degree of α.\nL100@P1: Proof. If α = αsep ◦ π\nL101@P1: n\nL102@P1: is inseparable, then # ker α = # ker αsep, since the kernel of π\nL103@P1: n\nL104@P1: is\nL105@P1: trivial (if we put π\nL106@P1: n\nL107@P1: in standard form ( u(x)\nL108@P1: v(x)\nL109@P1: ,\nL110@P1: s(x)\nL111@P1: t(x)\nL112@P1: y), then v(x) = 1 has no roots), and π\nL113@P1: n\nL114@P1: is surjective, by the previous theorem. Thus it is enough to consider the case where α is\nL115@P1: separable, which we now assume.\nL116@P1: Let α be in standard form ( u(x)\nL117@P1: v(x)\nL118@P1: ,\nL119@P1: s(x)\nL120@P1: t(x)\nL121@P1