9@P2: the scalar multiple nP = P +· · ·+P. This is clearly a group homomorphism, and, since the\nL160@P2: group operation is defined by rational functions, it is also a rational map and therefore an\nL161@P2: isogeny. It is an isogeny from E to itself (and therefore an endomorphism). We wish to put\nL162@P2: the isogeny [n] into standard form. In order to to this it turns out to be more convenient\nL163@P2: to work with weighted projective coordinates.\nL164@P2: 6.2 Jacobian coordinates\nL165@P2: Recall that points in standard projective coordinates are nonzero triples (x : y : z) subject\nL166@P2: to the equivalence relation\nL167@P2: (x : y : z) ∼ (λx : λy : λz) (for λ ∈ ¯k\nL168@P2: ∗\nL169@P2: ).\nL170@P2: We will instead work with the equivalence relation\nL171@P2: (x : y : z) ∼ (λ\nL172@P2: 2x : λ3\nL173@P2: y : λz) (for λ ∈ ¯k\nL174@P2: ∗\nL175@P2: ),\nL176@P2: which corresponds to assigning weights 2 and 3 to the variables x and y. Projective coor\u0002dinates with these weights are also called Jacobian coordinates. The homogeneous curve\nL177@P2: equation for E in Jacobian coordinates then has the form\nL178@P2: y\nL179@P2: 2 = x3 + Axz4 + Bz6\nL180@P2: ,\nL181@P2: which explains the motivation for giving x weight 2 and y weight 3: the leading terms\nL182@P2: for x and y do not involve z. In Jacobian coordinates, each point (x : y : z) with z 6= 0\nL183@P2: corresponds to the affine point (x/z2, y/z3), and the point at infinity is still (0 : 1 : 0).\nL184@P2: Remark 6.3. As an aside, the general Weierstrass form of an elliptic curve in Jacobian\nL185@P2: coordinates is\nL186@P2: y\nL187@P2: 2 + a1xyz + a3yz3 = x3 + a2x2\nL188@P2: z + a4xz4 + a6z\nL189@P2: 6\nL190@P2: ,\nL191@P2: which is a weighted homogeneous equation of degree 6. Each aiis the coefficient of the term\nL192@P2: with degree i in z. This explains the otherwise mysterious fact that there is no Weierstrass\nL193@P2: coefficient a5.\nL194@P2: 6.3 The group law in Jacobian coordinates\nL195@P2: 